INT. CHURCH BASEMENT - NIGHT

Gaslight hisses in brass sconces. Steam drifts off a copper kettle on a woodstove. Above, through the floorboards, the groan of an organ and a hundred muffled hymns. A winter wind rattles the high, narrow windows.

A ring of mismatched chairs. In it sit PAT PELLETIER, a rosy, round man in suspenders, and PATSY PELLETIER, his sister, in a bonnet, knitting something that is mostly knot, and DEACON FLINT, thin as a candlestick, who holds a hymnal like a shield.

Overhead, THE CHOIR launches into a hymn.

THE CHOIR (O.S.)
(muffled, soaring)
Rock of Ages, cleft for me!

PAT
Friends, we're going to begin.

PATSY
Pat, we've begun. You began when you sat down.

PAT
Then we're beginning again, Patsy.

DEACON FLINT
(to the room)
Is it the custom to speak all at once, or one by one?

PATSY
One by one, Deacon.

PAT
Though some of us never stop.

PATSY
I heard that.

PAT
You were meant to.

The kettle WHISTLES. Everyone turns.

DEACON FLINT
(muttering to himself)
Lord, give me strength or give me a drink.

PAT
Beg pardon, Deacon?

DEACON FLINT
Nothing. Hymn. I was humming a hymn.

PATSY
You were not humming.

THE CHOIR (O.S.)
Let me hide myself in Thee!

PAT
(to the room, loud)
Everyone, here's the rule. What's said under the floor stays under the floor.

PATSY
And what's said above?

PAT
Is the Lord's business, Patsy.

DEACON FLINT
Hold on. Patsy, you told me last week you'd quit.

PATSY
I did quit. Twice.

PAT
She quit on Tuesday and again on Wednesday.

PATSY
Because Tuesday didn't take!

DEACON FLINT
That's three drinks in three days, then.

PAT
Two. The third was medicinal.

PATSY
Pat!

The organ builds. The floorboards tremble. Dust sifts into the kettle steam.

DEACON FLINT
(rising)
I will not sit in a room of liars!

PAT
Sit down, Deacon!

PATSY
Pat, stop shouting!

DEACON FLINT
You're the ones who are shouting!

PAT
We are discussing!

THE CHOIR (O.S.)
Amen! AMEN!

The chandelier above them SWINGS. A jar of pickles rolls off a shelf and SMASHES. In the silence that follows, all three stare at the pickle brine spreading across the floor.

PATSY
(quietly)
Well. That's a sign.

PAT
Of what?

DEACON FLINT
That I'd like a pickle.
